L["darkness-cross"] = """It was about noon, and darkness came over the whole land until three in the afternoon, for the sun stopped shining. In the middle of the day, in the middle of the crucifixion, the sky went dark. The Old Testament prophet Amos had written about a day when God would make the sun go down at noon and darken the earth in broad daylight.

About three in the afternoon, Jesus cried out in a loud voice, Eloi, Eloi, lema sabachthani? which means, my God, my God, why have you forsaken me? These are the opening words of Psalm 22, a psalm that begins in anguish and ends in praise. He was feeling the full weight of what he had come to bear. Some of the bystanders heard him and thought he was calling Elijah.

Later, knowing that everything had now been finished, and so that Scripture would be fulfilled, Jesus said, I am thirsty. A jar of wine vinegar was there, so they soaked a sponge in it, put the sponge on a stalk of the hyssop plant, and lifted it to Jesus' lips. When he had received the drink, Jesus said, it is finished. Then he called out with a loud voice, Father, into your hands I commit my spirit. With that, he bowed his head and gave up his spirit.

It is finished. In the Greek, it is a single word. It was used in the ancient world when a debt was paid in full. The work Jesus came to do was complete. He had lived the life we could not live and borne the debt we could not pay.

At that moment the curtain of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom. This heavy curtain had separated the Most Holy Place, where God's presence dwelled, from the rest of the temple. Only the high priest could enter, once a year. Now it was torn from the top, as if by God's own hand. The way into God's presence was open. The earth shook, the rocks split, and the tombs broke open.

When the centurion, the Roman officer in charge of the crucifixion, and those with him saw what had happened, they were terrified and exclaimed, surely he was the Son of God! The crowd that had gathered went away beating their breasts. The women who had followed him from Galilee stood at a distance and watched.

Why does this matter? These are the most important hours in history. The darkness, the cry and the torn curtain all point to one thing. Jesus bore the judgment for sin, and the barrier between God and people was removed. And the first person to say who he was, was a Roman soldier.

What happened after? Joseph of Arimathea took the body down, and Jesus was buried. The disciples hid in fear. But the story was not over.

What does this mean for us today? When we feel abandoned by God, we are not the first. Jesus prayed the same words, and he knows what it is like. It also means that we don't need to earn our way to God. It is finished. Nothing is left for us to add. We can come with confidence, because the curtain has been torn.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus completed our salvation, and that the way to God is now open to all. It is finished."""

L["dead-christ"] = """Evening was approaching, and it was the day of Preparation, the day before the Sabbath. The Jewish law required that bodies not remain on crosses overnight during the Sabbath. A man named Joseph came from the Judean town of Arimathea. He was a rich man and a respected member of the Jewish council. Luke tells us he was a good and upright man, who had not consented to the council's decision and action. He was a secret disciple of Jesus, because he feared the Jewish leaders.

Now, however, Joseph found courage. He went boldly to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate was surprised to hear that Jesus was already dead. He called the centurion and made sure. When he learned it from the centurion, he gave the body to Joseph.

Joseph took the body down. He was joined by Nicodemus, the man who had first visited Jesus at night. Nicodemus brought a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about seventy-five pounds. Taking Jesus' body, the two of them wrapped it, with the spices, in strips of linen, as was the Jewish burial custom. It was a costly and loving act, the kind given to a king.

At the place where Jesus was crucified, there was a garden, and in the garden a new tomb, in which no one had yet been laid. It was Joseph's own tomb, cut out of rock. Because it was the Jewish day of Preparation and since the tomb was nearby, they laid Jesus there. Joseph rolled a big stone in front of the entrance to the tomb and went away.

Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were sitting there opposite the tomb. The women who had come with Jesus from Galilee followed Joseph and saw the tomb and how his body was laid in it. Then they went home and prepared spices and perfumes. But they rested on the Sabbath in obedience to the commandment.

The next day, the chief priests and the Pharisees went to Pilate. They remembered that Jesus had said that after three days he would rise again. So they asked that the tomb be made secure. Pilate said, take a guard. Make the tomb as secure as you know how. So they went and made the tomb secure by putting a seal on the stone and posting the guard.

Why does this matter? Two respected men who had been afraid to speak up came forward in the darkest hour. They stood up when the twelve had run away. And the burial confirms that Jesus really died. It was a real death and a real tomb, so the resurrection would be a real resurrection. Isaiah had said that he would be assigned a grave with the wicked and with the rich in his death. It was fulfilled in Joseph's tomb.

What happened after? The disciples spent a quiet and heartbroken Sabbath. They thought it was over. But on the third day, early in the morning, the women came to the tomb and found the stone rolled away.

What does this mean for us today? Sometimes God's silence is not his absence. The Saturday between the cross and the empty tomb was the longest day for his followers, and God was still at work. It also shows that it is never too late to step out of the shadows and act on your faith. Joseph and Nicodemus found their courage when it mattered.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus truly died for us, and that even the grave could not hold him. And it sets the stage for the greatest morning in history."""
